## Explanation
In C, a file is a resource managed by the operating system. Think of opening a file like checking out a book from a library: the library gives you a "file pointer" (a library card) to keep track of that specific book. Every time you open a file, the system gives you one of these pointers, but there is a strict limit to how many you can hold at once. If you keep checking out books without ever returning them, the library will eventually run out of cards and refuse to give you any more, causing your program to crash or fail.

## Use Cases
Opening files in a loop is often used when processing batches of data, such as reading logs from hundreds of different sensors or processing thousands of individual image files. It is vital to close these files immediately after you are finished with them to "return the library card" so the system can reuse that resource for the next task.

